# Environments
# Deny every .env variant, then re-allow ONLY what is safe to commit. A bare
# `.env` rule does NOT cover `.env.production` -- which is exactly the file that
# holds the production database password and SECRET_KEY.
#
# Order matters: git applies the LAST matching pattern, so the broad `.env.*`
# denial must come before the `!` re-allows.
#
# COMMITTED (safe):
#   .env.example, .env.production.example  -- templates, placeholders only
#   .env.staging.enc, .env.production.enc  -- AES-256-CBC ciphertext; committing
#                                             these is the entire point of the
#                                             env-encryption pattern
# NEVER COMMITTED:
#   .env             -- what actually lands on the VPS
#   .env.staging     -- plaintext working copy
#   .env.production  -- plaintext working copy
#   .env.key         -- the AES key itself. Committing this alongside the .enc
#                       files would make the encryption worthless.
#
# Verify after editing (all six must behave):
#   git check-ignore -v .env .env.staging .env.production .env.key
#   git check-ignore -v .env.staging.enc .env.production.enc   # must NOT match
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example
!.env.staging.enc
!.env.production.enc
.venv
env/
venv/
ENV/
env.bak/
venv.bak/

# Poetry
# poetry.lock is INTENTIONALLY TRACKED: it is what makes container builds and CI
# reproducible. Do not re-ignore it.

